A||||9||AkursedX||The Thing I hate most about Fieros||||8||
Z||000000||AkursedX||08-02-2001||08:06 PM||||Ok, I am sure some of you people will agree with me...<P>Well, I got my new 87GT home and the first thing I need to do is sandblast the cradle and the spaceframe in the rear.  So, this results in having to remove the wheel-well.<P>Well I want to know what F*CKING moron chose to put those stupid little screw-in push tabs!!!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif">  They NEVER come out when you try to unscrew them, and if you decide to rip them out, you end up breaking them!!  If there is one thing that I hate about Fieros, it has to be that.<P>On a side note, I found that the best way to get those things out when they won't unscrew is to jab a pair of needle-mose pliers into the enge of them and pull them out.  Seems to work pretty well.<P>On another side note, I got me some pretty big holes in the upper trunk area.  Anyone have any idea on how to patch them up??<P>Well thank you all for listening  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigpic2_2.jpg"> ||||reg||8
Z||000001||FieroJoe||08-02-2001||11:39 PM||||Yup, I have to agree with those stupid little things too.  I used my door panel removal tool to get them out, and still broke some.<P>Sounds like you have the common trunk rust.  All Fiero's are prone to this.  Just grind away all the rusted part, and weld in a new piece.  Make sure to get a good weld all the way around, and you might even want to use some seam sealer to seal up the welds even better.  Then, use some spray on undercoating on both the inside and outside, for further rust protection.||||reg||1
Z||000002||AkursedX||08-02-2001||11:42 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroJoe:<BR><B><P>Sounds like you have the common trunk rust.  All Fiero's are prone to this.  Just grind away all the rusted part, and weld in a new piece.  Make sure to get a good weld all the way around, and you might even want to use some seam sealer to seal up the welds even better.  Then, use some spray on undercoating on both the inside and outside, for further rust protection.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well I was planning on doing everything but the seam sealer.  I should do that as well.  I'm really looking foward to getting all the sandblasting done so I can get to the welding  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  I like playing with fire  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/cool.gif"><P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Jim Jimenez||08-02-2001||11:52 PM||||If you need some new stupid little screw retainers, visit your local GM dealer and ask for part #20664092. He'll fix you up with a bag of 20 if you need 'em. <P>Happy Fieroing!!||||reg||7
Z||000004||EdsB52||08-03-2001||12:34 AM||||What's rust? (look at the State)||||reg||1
Z||000005||AkursedX||08-03-2001||12:47 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by EdsB52:<BR><B>What's rust? (look at the State)</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE> <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif">  Actually, my 88GT is virtually rust-free, so this whole restoring my Fiero is a new experience for me  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Ken Wittlief||08-03-2001||10:08 AM||||those little plastic fasteners were designed with the assembly line in mind. Ill bet they could install the wheel well liner in about 9 seconds flat<P>  and Im sure they figured if a dealer has to pull them out, they would replace em with new ones.<P>  If they had put nice stainless bolts and captive stainless nuts on the other side on the car, the car would cost about $100 more to produce.<P> Its frustrating for us now, but when you see stuff like this the reason for it is almost always the assembly line.||||reg||11
Z||000007||StuGood||08-03-2001||10:23 AM||||Actually, I <B>like</B> the little push-button fasteners - they hold tight, don't rust, don't strip like screws might (okay, <B>would</B>, in a salt-and-crud area like the wheel well).  GM probably likes them, too, because they install in a second, with no tools.  I like them for that reason as well.<P>But I have to agree that the ones with the little "Philips" head cut in the button are useless.  On my own car, I replace those with the plain button type if available.  <P>I use a little "mini pry bar" / nail puller to remove the pushbutton fasteners.  Pry the button outward as far as it goes, then put the prybar under the head of the fastener and pull it out.  Easy as 3.14159...!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Getting the "screwheaded pushbutton" ones out sometimes means just unscrewing the button and prying the fastener out, as I'm pretty sure was the original intent.  But at least half the time, have to wedge a knifeblade under the screwhead, and then use needlenose pliers to gently pull/twist the button out of there.  By then the little plastic screw is toast, and somebody's "great idea" lands in the trash barrel  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">.  If I shoot straight, that is  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">.   ||||reg||1
Z||000008||KRMFiero||08-03-2001||10:48 AM||||I hate them things, I use jumper cables to get mine out<P>Kyle||||reg||1
Z||000009||dlancer407||08-04-2001||11:00 AM||||The little fasteners you spoke of are a real pain, but they don't rust which is a real plus.<P>As far as the rust goes, my 85 GT has the same problem. I used to use spray undercoating when fixing rusted areas. With the Fiero I bought a couple of cans of spray  in bedliner. This seems to work much better. It dries completely,not tacky when dry, and it dries really fast, and it is much more durable. It does cost about twice as much for a can but I think it is really worth it.||||reg||6
